  "competitor_analysis": {
    "title": "Competitor Analysis",
    "content": "The competitive landscape for an AI Tutor for MBA Students in the United States includes both direct and indirect alternatives. General-purpose AI tools, such as ChatGPT, are already widely used by students (66% usage) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37] and could serve as readily available, potentially free, substitutes for a dedicated AI tutor. Traditional academic support services, including human tutoring, university academic support centers, and peer study groups, represent established alternatives that MBA students may utilize. Furthermore, universities are actively developing and implementing AI policies, and some may develop or endorse their own AI learning tools, potentially requiring specific certifications for external solutions [web-4c21a24af9ac4d20b5a44bf7a0119335]. Other EdTech companies are also likely developing AI-powered learning tools that could compete for the attention of MBA students. The low perception of AI's benefits in education among the US public (only 39% consider it beneficial) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37] and the adoption gap among higher education faculty (only 22% regularly use generative AI) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37] suggest that overcoming existing habits and perceptions will be a significant competitive challenge.",
